  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 302 AM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThe bulk of our precipitation from the cold front passing through is \nmoving farther into Wyoming early this morning, but a bit does \nlinger in the higher elevations of the upper Snake River Plain and \nEastern Highlands. We even had a convective line of rain/snow mix \nmove through the Monida Pass area with some lightning this morning. \nBut as this trough and cold front leave, we still have another weak \nshortwave passing through later today. This won't bring another well-\norganized line through Eastern Idaho, but it will keep isolated \nprecipitation chances around, especially for this evening. Overall, \nadditional QPF today will stay below a tenth of an inch. Behind the \ncold front, highs today will be in the 40s to low 50s for most. \nWinds remain breezy in the upper Snake River Plain today with gusts \naround 20 to 30 mph. Wednesday will be nearly a repeat of \ntoday, though drier as we briefly return to southwesterly flow \naloft ahead of the next system as temperatures warm into the \nupper 40s to mid 50s on Thursday afternoon. \n\nAn area of low pressure off of the coast of the PNW brings moisture \ninto Eastern Idaho for the weekend, but will begin to bring \nprecipitation back to us as early as overnight Thursday into Friday. \nDeep troughing over much of the West pulls Pacific moisture into \nIdaho on Friday, Saturday, and Sunday. This will keep our highs in \nthe 40s and 50s on Saturday, and 40s for Sunday and Monday. We will \ncontinue to see how precipitation totals change over the next \nseveral model runs, so stay tuned for those amounts as we get a \nlittle closer to it.",
